{{History of Balisca}}
The '''Maroon Confederacy''' ({{lang-bl|Confederación cimarróna}}; {{langx|mxi|Kúńfidirasiuń ḡárnata|lit=stranger's confederation}}), also called '''Satanazes''' ("Isle of Devils") in some early historical writings, was a revolutionary polity that brought together self-liberated slaves, indigenous people, and pirates and convicts, amongst others against [[Colonial Balisca|colonial]] authority of the [[Volisanian Empire]]. Originally confined to the [[Marañón basin]], the violent upheaval resulted in a substantial migration of people south into [[Araucaria|''Pucará'']], where it [[Maroon Wars|expanded exponentially]] and adopted [[W:Guerrilla warfare|guerrilla warfare]] to resist colonial incursions.  

This diverse resistance gradually developed a society and government that adopted a range of {{W|Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous}} and [[West Kaftia|West Kaftian]] socio-political models, with aspects of [[W:Sephardic Jews|Sephardic]] and [[Mozarabia|Moros]] culture and specifically clan adaptations. It was decentralized in nature, operating as a {{W|confederation}} of nominally independent towns and settlements united by their common goal of defending against the threat posed by the Volisans and undermining the slave trade. Between the outbreak of the first slave revolts in 1593, largely in [[Bayara]], and the outbreak of the [[Baliscan War of Independence]] in 1809, more than 375,000 formerly enslaved and free-born black people who faced social and legal oppression in the [[Ethiopic world]], escaped to Pucará. 

[[Daramazán]] was founded as a colony 1617 by escaped Moro convicts and Kaftian slaves, at a time when the [[Marañón basin]] remained largely unexplored by Illypnians. As a refuge for those fleeing the colonial authorities, the territory became notorious for its gaudy displays of wealth and loose morals. It was a popular homeport for [[Maroon pirates]] from which to launch their raids on slave ships and settlements to liberate enslaved people. The outbreak of slave revolts against the Illypnians was to an extent supported by the colony, which helped to smuggle individuals along the Araucarian Passage. This city later joined with the other maroon settlements to form the Confederacy.

Eight Volisan naval expeditions invaded the territory in an attempt to conquer it between 1699 and 1804, but these all failed with considerable loss of life as a result of violent guerrilla tactics and a lack of familiarity with the land. The Confederacy later intervened in the [[Baliscan Civil War]], playing a decisive role in beating back the [[Siege of Castejón]], and in subsequent years developed an amiable relationship with [[Anyos Federales|Reconstructionist Balisca]] with the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]]. The Confederacy continued to exercise sovereignty until it confederated with the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] at the [[1883 Confederal Convention]].

